# Feature maps require substantial new features

Applies to changes under `maps/`, especially `maps/feature-map/` and its
same-named agent-access companions.

## Requirements

- Add a feature recipe, capability ID, sub-feature, entry point, or descriptive
  expansion only for a substantial new user-facing feature: a distinct,
  meaningful task that users could not previously perform and that an existing
  capability does not already cover. A new workflow such as reusable scheduled
  routines qualifies; an extra sort option in an existing task list does not.
- Leave maps unchanged for routine bug fixes, small behavior adjustments, UI or
  copy polish, additional filters or settings within an existing capability,
  refactors, performance work, logging, and test changes. Code changes alone do
  not require a map update, regardless of how many files they touch.
- Do not append implementation details, edge-case narratives, regression notes,
  test results, or PR summaries to existing recipes to document small updates.
  Keep those in the relevant tests, technical docs, PR, or issue.
- Keep additions focused on the new feature and reuse existing capability IDs
  when they already cover the work. Do not expand unrelated recipes or companion
  files. When the feature threshold is unclear, leave the maps unchanged.

## Existing-entry corrections

Instructions to keep maps synchronized require a minimal correction only when an
existing entry would become materially inaccurate, such as a removed entry
point, changed access requirement, or obsolete user journey. Correct or remove
the affected text in place and preserve stable IDs; this does not justify new
capabilities or incidental detail. Explicitly requested map maintenance may fix
existing entries but must apply the same threshold to additions.

## Review

For every addition, identify the substantial new user capability and why an
existing entry cannot cover it. Reject additions for minor updates and unrelated
map edits. For corrections, check that the edit fixes a concrete inaccuracy and
stays limited to the affected entry.
